  "live_records": {
    "q-96281": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96281",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that the continuous random variable $V$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-2,0],$ compute $P(V\\le -\\frac{1}{2}).$",
      "worked_solution": "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $V$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(v)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le v\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $V$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-2,0],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(v)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2},\\, & -2\\le v\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V\\le -\\frac{1}{2}) & =P(-2\\le V\\le -\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1/2}f(v)\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1/2}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}-(-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
