      "dom_id": "step-q130540",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given that the continuous random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-5,-2],$ compute $P(X<-4).$",
      "worked_solution": "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-5,-2],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3},\\, & -5\\le x\\le -2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-4) & =P(-5\\le X<-4) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-(-5)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
