    "q-90609": {
      "dom_id": "step-q90609",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Given that the continuous random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-4,3],$ compute $P(X<-2).$",
      "worked_solution": "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-4,3],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7},\\, & -4\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-2) & =P(-4<X<-2) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{-2}\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-(-4)}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
